{
  "what": "Common English words, lower case, for the narration text pass's emphasis test.",
  "why": "A run of capitals is read in ordinary case ONLY when the lower-cased form is an English word — the author shouted a word. Without a word test the reading was offered to every acronym, and OSCE, RSHA, SHAEF, BOAC, ICAO, IATA, ASEAN, SWAPO, UNITA, FRELIMO, COMECON, UNPROFOR, ELAS, EOKA and ODESSA all accepted it (the fifth adversarial review, 2026-09-04).",
  "source": "Compiled for this repository from standard high-frequency English vocabulary. It is NOT derived from any third-party word list, so no third-party licence applies; it is covered by this repository's own licence.",
  "policy": "A POSITIVE list. A word that is not here is refused the lower-cased reading and offered the spaced-letters reading instead, which is the safe direction: the cost of a miss is an unconverted emphasis, and the cost of a false accept is a wrong word written into the user's book. Adding a word is a deliberate act, exactly as it is for the abbreviation table. Acronyms that happen to be English words (ARMS, IRAQ, MASH) keep both readings by this test; that is accepted.",
